My name is Rohit Talreja,
and in this session,

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.280
we'll be covering
Google Cloud's security,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.220
privacy, and compliance
solutions for health care.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.205
I am a product manager
on the Health Care

00:00:17.205 --> 00:00:19.140
and Life Sciences Team
within Google Cloud,

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.380
and my primary focus areas
are artificial intelligence,

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.140
security, and governance.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.290
So before we get into
any specific technologies

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.200
and solutions, it actually
helps quite a bit to deep dive

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.320
into the health care
and life sciences market

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.620
to understand the regulatory
landscape and the challenges

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.180
that organizations are facing.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.470
So if we look at one overview
study on the regulatory burden

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:47.040
that hospitals and
health systems face,

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.910
they calculated a significant
financial and time cost

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:52.330
to compliance.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:54.870
So in this report,
regulatory compliance

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:59.340
is not just the security
and privacy-related aspects.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:04.200
It also covers areas like
documentation, patient safety,

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.170
and others.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:08.850
So if we look at just IT
compliance, specifically

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.190
the information
security, privacy,

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.490
compliance documentation
of the infrastructure,

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:16.590
that cost is still
a major component,

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.800
but it's not everything
that you see on this slide.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.810
Looking more closely
at the figures

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.960
themselves, across the industry,
health care organizations

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.730
are spending approximately $39
billion a year in aggregate

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:33.360
just on the administrative
activities of compliance.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.430
This presents a huge
financial burden

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.830
to the organizations
who need to be focused

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:44.160
on providing care and optimizing
their infrastructure as well.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.340
The financial cost isn't
the only thing, however.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.750
There are also approximately 59
full-time employee equivalents

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.000
dedicated to
regulatory compliance

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.010
within an
average-sized hospital.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.910
This includes approximately
15 or about a quarter of which

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.210
our clinical staff, such
as doctors and nurses.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:06.360
Looking at the patient impact
of both the financial aspects

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.750
and the clinician aspects,
the cost of compliance results

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.960
in material time taken
away from patient care

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.960
and even potentially
creates redundant efforts

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.920
within an organization.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.138
So when we look through the
Cloud provider technology, one

00:02:21.138 --> 00:02:22.680
of the things that
we think about is,

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.170
how can Cloud technologies
and Cloud services

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:29.220
help reduce this
burden of compliance?

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.720
Now, where does this compliance
requirements come from?

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.060
Unfortunately, there
isn't one single answer

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.730
that we can tackle that
addresses all compliance

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.070
requirements, all security
and privacy requirements

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:42.510
for an organization.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.240
Instead, we have to look
at kind of the amalgamation

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.890
of different sorts of regulatory
frameworks, industry standards,

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.800
and other pressures
health care organizations

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.330
face for compliance.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:58.840
So the first thing to look into
is data privacy regulation.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.890
This is highly specific
to a particular region

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:07.630
such as the US, the EU, or even
specific states or counties

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:08.730
or provinces.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.760
However, in general,
data privacy regulation

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.550
is a legal requirement
relating to data collection,

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:18.210
processing, and sharing.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:23.070
The penalties for breaking
these data privacy regulations

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.090
also range.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.690
They can vary from
financial penalties

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.060
to lawsuits to criminal
cases, depending

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.420
on the specific violation,
how severe it was,

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.980
and what regulation
is in question.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:37.800
And finally, because
these regulations

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.560
are meant to stand
through the test of time,

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.990
they're generally
not descriptive.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.850
You won't see specific
regulations say

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:50.520
a specific key lens
for encryption or exact

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.490
technologies to use,
but instead, they're

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.410
generally more descriptive.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.630
They'll say things like
data must be protected

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.500
in the following ways.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:02.400
And often, there's no
official certification

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.430
for these regulations.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:05.520
Instead, organizations
have to ensure

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.470
that they're aligning
to the letter

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:09.690
and spirit of the
regulation, but there

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.720
is room for some
organization-specific

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:13.990
interpretation.

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:17.310
So if we look at just one or
two examples of data privacy

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:20.560
regulation, in the US, the
one that most health care

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.730
organizations are
familiar with is HIPAA.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.790
And HIPAA is a regulation,
again, for the US

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:31.170
that covers the use, disclosure,
and security of protected

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.430
health information.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:36.360
And protected health information
is a specific defined category

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.570
in HIPAA, but essentially,
it's patient information.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:43.050
Information relating to the
care of the patient, any bills

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.720
they receive, et cetera.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.240
For HIPAA specifically,
it applies

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.480
to two types of organizations
in the US-- covered entities

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:52.540
and business associates.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:57.090
And these categories just define
how health care organizations

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:57.780
operate.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.170
Covered entities are
generally the point of care.

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.510
The insurance companies,
the health care data

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:04.710
clearinghouses.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:08.190
And then the business associates
are any vendors or partners

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.500
of the covered entity
that may do data

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:14.190
processing, storage, or
transmission on behalf

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:15.780
of the covered entity.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:18.570
Again, as mentioned, there's
no official certification

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:22.470
for HIPAA, and the requirements
are not very prescriptive.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:24.570
However, they do give
a general overview

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:28.090
of how data should be used,
collected, protected, stored,

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:29.610
and so on.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.420
There's another
example of this--

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:35.880
one in the EU that covers
the entire EU jurisdiction

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.610
and covers some
foreign jurisdiction

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:42.920
where EU citizen data may
be processed or transmitted.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:45.990
So in GDPR, again, you'll see
there is a parallel to HIPAA

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.210
in that it covers the
scope, processing,

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:50.070
and transfer of personal data.

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:53.250
In this case, personal
data includes some aspects

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.230
of health data as well.

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:59.382
The GDPR chart does not use
the same language as HIPAA,

00:05:59.382 --> 00:06:03.840
but it says GDPR is applicable
to data controllers and data

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:05.010
processors.

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:07.530
Again, there isn't an exact
parallel to covered entities

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.510
and business
associates under HIPAA,

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.160
but you can tell
that they're trying

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.210
to segment the categories
of people who have collected

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.480
and have ownership of
the data versus people

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.590
who have temporary access
for a specific case.

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:28.140
And finally, again, there's
no certification for GDPR,

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:33.760
and organizations have to kind
of align with its guidance.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.340
Going into the next
topic, the next topic

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:37.630
is government frameworks.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:39.220
You can think of
these as requirements

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:42.490
for contracting with
government agencies.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:45.250
The penalties here
are not usually

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:49.880
as severe as for the
data privacy regulation.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.420
However, they can
still be very material.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:56.170
Breach of government frameworks
can jeopardize contracts

00:06:56.170 --> 00:07:00.070
with government agencies,
or it can result in lawsuits

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:02.520
to recoup any damages.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.480
Here, the certification
requirements

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.640
are generally a bit
more prescriptive.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:08.980
We'll go into a couple
of examples later.

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:11.260
But unlike the data
privacy regulation

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:13.600
which contained
general guidance,

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.160
government frameworks
tend to be very specific.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.300
They can have hundreds or
thousands of specific controls

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:23.050
that an organization has to
implement, test, and document,

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:26.200
and there are different
degrees of compliance

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:28.090
depending on the framework.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.580
So one common
example here, again,

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:35.410
for a US-centric view of
things is called FedRAMP.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:38.740
And FedRAMP is a US government
requirement for Cloud service

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:43.180
providers specifically in order
to allow the government agency

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:46.030
to purchase Cloud
provider solutions.

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:48.820
And FedRAMP tends to
be very prescriptive,

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:51.890
and there are multiple
levels of compliance.

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:54.280
So organizations
can meet a number

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:56.890
of different bars of
FedRAMP compliance

00:07:56.890 --> 00:08:01.960
which allows them to
operate certain workloads

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:04.240
or sell certain
levels of technology

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:07.700
to the US government agencies.

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.630
There are a lot of government
frameworks depending

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:12.610
on where in the world
you are, which government

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:14.350
agencies you're working with.

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:19.330
Another example that comes up is
the UK National Health Service

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:20.320
toolkits.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.960
And these are guidelines
for technology outsourcing.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.030
They're a little bit less
prescriptive than FedRAMP,

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:32.770
but still more prescriptive
than data privacy regulation.

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:34.437
There is no official
certification here.

00:08:34.437 --> 00:08:36.312
So you'll see that
government frameworks tend

00:08:36.312 --> 00:08:38.500
to be a little bit more
varied on whether they

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:43.380
have official
certification path or not.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:47.100
Another category of requirements
comes from industry standards,

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.570
and industry
standards tend to be

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:52.920
committee-driven for a
specific market or use case.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:56.190
Most require an independent
third party audit.

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:59.340
It's not something that you
can generally self-attest to

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:00.320
within an organization.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:03.630
There has to be some sort of
checks and balances in place.

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.840
And most importantly,
participation

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:10.020
in industry standards
is voluntary in general.

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:13.110
But it's often enforced
on a contractual basis

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:16.210
between a vendor
and their customer.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:21.150
So there's no specific penalties
like there are for data privacy

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:23.940
regulation that you'd get from
the government specifically,

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:27.540
but you may be susceptible
to specific agreements

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:30.040
with your vendors
or your customers,

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:31.860
depending on what
industry standards are

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:34.060
in scope for that relationship.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:36.060
There are a few examples here.

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:39.750
Most people are generally
familiar with the SOC family

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:41.440
of standards.

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:43.290
And these are
globally recognized

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:46.800
standards for IT operations,
security, availability,

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:51.580
confidentiality,
integrity, and so on.

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:54.150
This is a very general
purpose standard

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:57.030
that's useful for a lot
of technology providers

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:58.780
across different domains.

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:01.830
And then we can look at two
specific industry standard

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:06.878
examples, one for health care
and one for financial services.

00:10:06.878 --> 00:10:08.670
Obviously, these aren't
mutually exclusive.

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:11.430
There are financial
services organizations

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:14.050
that operate in the
health care space.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:18.240
So it's important to understand
that the domain of the industry

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:21.090
standard does not
necessarily map one-to-one

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:24.450
with the organizations that
have to comply with it as part

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:26.190
of their business obligations.

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:32.360
And the last thing to
touch upon in this session

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.850
is good practice regulations.

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:37.340
And good practice
or GXP regulations

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:41.330
are actually a combination of
government regulation, industry

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:45.470
standard, tribal knowledge,
and other organization-specific

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:46.410
controls.

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:51.080
They tend to be more nebulous
for specific use cases.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:54.230
However, some examples that are
relevant to the health and life

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:56.930
sciences industry are
good clinical practice,

00:10:56.930 --> 00:11:00.720
good laboratory practice, and
good manufacturing practice.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.410
There are some
common considerations

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:05.600
for these various GXPs.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.000
Often these-- they
have similarities

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:11.510
in the configuration validation
that organizations have to do,

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:14.120
the contingency planning
process that organizations

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:15.300
have to have in place.

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:18.440
If a disaster were to
occur, as well as access

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.390
control and audit
trails to make sure

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:23.060
that any access to the system
is properly documented.

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:26.480
Any changes are properly
validated and documented

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:27.720
as well.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:30.080
So this is essentially
the summary

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:33.170
of all of the various
regulatory and industry standard

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:38.840
frameworks that make up the IT
side of health care compliance.

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:41.810
And if you take a
closer look and distill

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:44.750
all of these various regulations
and other requirements

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:46.730
relevant to the
industry, you'll see

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:49.400
that there are quite a
lot of commonalities.

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:52.160
And this slide is
meant to indicate

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:55.370
some of these commonalities that
health care organizations are

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:58.970
facing when it comes to setting
up an infrastructure and IT

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:00.030
program.

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:03.560
So at the infrastructure
level, the physical machines,

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:05.780
the virtual machines,
the software running

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:08.630
on the virtual machines, it's
important that all of them,

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:10.880
all of these various
pieces, are appropriately

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:14.120
hardened to protect
against vulnerabilities

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:18.690
that may be appropriate for
that level of the stack.

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:21.090
So if we think about
network security,

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:23.210
one of the common attacks
that can take place

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:25.130
is a denial of service attack.

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:27.890
And protecting this
at the network level

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:30.260
is appropriate because it
is fundamentally a network

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:32.660
and scaling type of attack.

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:36.860
At the data protection layer,
one of the common protections

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:38.050
is encryption.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:39.800
And encryption is
applicable at this layer

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:41.800
because you want to make
sure that the data that

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:44.030
resides on disk, the data
that's being transmitted

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:48.620
between various services and
even your backup systems,

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:51.170
has the proper levels
of encryption to protect

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:54.240
against vulnerabilities
at the data storage level.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:56.660
And then if you keep
moving up the stack,

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:59.540
a lot of the industry standards
and regulatory frameworks

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:04.100
also have recommendations around
identity and access management,

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:07.460
endpoint security, how you
allow external users to connect

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:12.195
to your systems, how you record
accesses by external users,

00:13:12.195 --> 00:13:13.820
and make sure there's
a permanent audit

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:17.860
trail, and so on and so forth.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:21.270
So at this point, it's important
to kind of transition to see,

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:21.770
OK.

00:13:21.770 --> 00:13:25.030
How do we move from the
general scope of requirements

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:27.940
into something that
is specific to Google

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:30.280
Cloud, and specifically,
implementing

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:34.628
secure private and compliant
workloads on Google Cloud?

00:13:34.628 --> 00:13:36.420
The first thing that's
important to discuss

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:39.150
when I'm talking about Cloud
is the shared responsibility

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.540
model, and specifically, the
shared responsibility model

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:43.620
in detail.

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:46.710
The shared
responsibility model says

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:51.630
that in contrast to
traditional IT systems

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:55.110
where the IT owner is
responsible for maintaining

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:59.310
the infrastructure stack top
to bottom, with Cloud Services,

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:01.770
that responsibility is
shared between the Cloud

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:05.610
provider and the in-house
IT team of the organization.

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:08.040
And it's beneficial
from multiple angles.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:11.040
The shared responsibility
model allows each organization

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:15.330
to focus on their specific
projects and core competencies,

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:17.040
while Google, the
Cloud provider,

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:20.610
focuses on managing the
services and the underlying

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:21.730
infrastructure.

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:23.950
And this can be from
a security standpoint,

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:27.780
from a scaling standpoint, from
a freshness of the IT stack,

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:30.450
so to speak, making sure that
the machines are up to date

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:32.550
and replaced periodically.

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:36.370
And there are other benefits
from this model as well.

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:38.100
It allows organizations
to benefit

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:41.070
from Google's world-class
security and privacy teams

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:44.070
who are in charge of
maintaining the infrastructure.

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:46.980
And you can also take
advantage of the cost savings

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:50.670
that stem from Google's ability
to purchase these technologies

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:54.780
in bulk and efficiently manage
the data center environments

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:57.690
and delivering the
Cloud services.

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:01.530
So on this diagram, you can
see that different levels

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:05.190
of services have different
levels of responsibility

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:08.860
related to security,
privacy, and compliance.

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:11.190
And this is generally
a function of how much

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:15.910
control the Cloud service
user has over the environment.

00:15:15.910 --> 00:15:19.140
So in an Infrastructure
As a Service or IAS

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:20.850
service, you'll
see that there is

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:25.050
a fair amount of responsibility
that the customers IT

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:26.730
team still has to manage.

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:29.550
But this is also reflected
in the level of control

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:31.990
that their IT team
has over the system.

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:35.160
So if there are requirements
to have a specific guest OS

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:38.900
image, a specific
network topography,

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:42.870
a specific level of encryption
using specific key lens

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:46.920
specific encryption algorithms,
that level of control

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:51.120
comes with the comparable
level of responsibility

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:53.280
to do things appropriately.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:56.370
And therefore, you'll see that
the Cloud provider, Google,

00:15:56.370 --> 00:16:01.320
cannot manage as much of the
system as they can in other

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:02.470
services.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:04.950
So when we move
kind of up the stack

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:07.440
into more managed services,
let's say a platform

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:09.330
as a service
offering, you can see

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:12.270
that the level of responsibility
managed by the Cloud provider

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:13.360
increases.

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:15.780
And this is to reflect that
the Cloud provider is now

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:18.360
taking more of the
decisions and responsibility

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:20.730
around scaling
security compliance

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:24.580
and providing a managed
service to the user.

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:26.550
And finally, at the
right-hand side,

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:28.890
with the software as
a service application,

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:31.260
the Cloud provider has
taken on a majority

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:34.860
of the responsibility in
maintaining the service,

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:36.870
and then the user
only generally has

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:39.960
to worry about what data they
put in the application, who

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:43.240
they share access with,
and so on and so forth.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:46.180
So there is a column
missing in this diagram,

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:49.080
and that's kind of the
traditional IT model before

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:51.810
Cloud services and
that would be where

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:55.740
an organization's in-house IT
team or outsourced IT team was

00:16:55.740 --> 00:17:00.340
responsible for all of these
activities related to Cloud--

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:02.070
to the security of the platform.

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:06.190
And that would be a fully
blue column, so to speak.

00:17:06.190 --> 00:17:10.470
So you can see that even in the
most flexible Cloud services,

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:13.290
there is still a decent
amount of responsibility

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:16.069
that the Cloud
provider is managing.

00:17:16.069 --> 00:17:20.240
So let's take a look at some
specific examples related

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:21.200
to Google Cloud.

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:23.930
If we look at a hypothetical
shared responsibility

00:17:23.930 --> 00:17:26.930
matrix for Compute
Engine, which is

00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:30.390
our infrastructure as a
service compute platform,

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:33.080
you'll see that at the
bottom of the stack,

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:36.590
Google takes full responsibility
for the hardware underlying

00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:37.260
the service.

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:41.280
And we implement that using
our proprietary hardware stack.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:45.230
We also take responsibility for
the boot process, the kernel,

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:48.950
the storage and encryption at
the physical layer, as well as

00:17:48.950 --> 00:17:51.530
the networking, again,
on the physical side.

00:17:51.530 --> 00:17:54.380
And this is done through
Google's proprietary technology

00:17:54.380 --> 00:17:57.680
and hardware stack to make sure
that the vulnerabilities are

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:01.820
mitigated, attacks can be
prevented and/or mitigated,

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:04.520
and that Google has
taken responsibility

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:08.360
for the security and
compliance of these components.

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:11.810
Turning focus to the
customer responsibilities,

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:14.510
they start pretty much at the--

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:17.910
anything that is installed
on the virtual machine.

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:22.130
So the guest OS, the choice
of OS is with the user.

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:24.680
What data they put on the
machine is up to the user.

00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:26.690
How they configure the network.

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:29.330
How they authenticate
users to the system.

00:18:29.330 --> 00:18:32.390
Basically, how they scale the
virtual machines up and down

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:34.610
depending on traffic
going to the application,

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:37.290
and then all the way up
to the top of the stack,

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:40.720
what web services run
on the virtual machine.

00:18:40.720 --> 00:18:42.860
So there's a lot
of freedom afforded

00:18:42.860 --> 00:18:46.490
to the users of Compute
Engine to configure and manage

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:48.080
their applications
appropriately,

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:49.970
but that also comes
with the responsibility

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:52.490
to do it correctly.

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:56.000
Transitioning to a platform
service such as BigQuery,

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:58.820
you can automatically see
that Google's responsibility

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:01.610
has moved up the
stack, so to speak.

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:03.950
Google is now
responsible specifically

00:19:03.950 --> 00:19:08.600
for maintaining the
operations of the service.

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:11.270
And what that means
is BigQuery scales

00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:13.130
automatically for
the amount of data

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:15.360
that customers put
in the platform.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:18.290
So Google is handling adding
more memory to the system.

00:19:18.290 --> 00:19:19.280
Adding more storage.

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:21.230
Adding more compute capacity.

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:22.850
And customers do not have to.

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:26.600
But there is still customer
responsibility here,

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:28.220
and the responsibility
again goes

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:33.020
from which users have access
to these BigQuery instances

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:34.970
and BigQuery data
sets all the way up

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:41.960
to what applications are able
to manipulate data in BigQuery.

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:44.450
Going to the far
end of the spectrum,

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:47.880
an example of a SAS platform in
Google Cloud is Google Drive.

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:52.040
And a hypothetical shared
responsibility matrix for this

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:53.930
shows that Google's
responsibility

00:19:53.930 --> 00:19:56.270
moves quite far up the stack.

00:19:56.270 --> 00:20:00.410
Google is now responsible for
deploying the service in a way

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:03.440
that customers can
appropriately access it,

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:05.240
but customers,
again, will always

00:20:05.240 --> 00:20:08.000
retain some level
of responsibility

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:11.390
for choosing what content
they put into the service, who

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:14.510
is able to access it within
the customer's organization,

00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:16.470
and so on.

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:20.120
So if we summarize
Google's technology suite

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:23.690
for helping customers with
their shared responsibilities,

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:27.530
you'll see that Google has
products and services to help

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:31.710
with shared responsibilities
in all of these areas.

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:33.470
So on the infrastructure
side, there

00:20:33.470 --> 00:20:36.020
are things like
shielded VMs, which

00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:38.690
can help protect the
infrastructure as a service

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:39.560
offerings.

00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:44.330
There are services like data
loss prevention and VPC Service

00:20:44.330 --> 00:20:46.670
Controls that can help
these platform as a service

00:20:46.670 --> 00:20:48.920
services manage their
data appropriately

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:51.320
and access controls
appropriately.

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:53.780
And then finally, at
the top of the stack,

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:55.850
even with regards
to SAS services,

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:58.820
there are endpoint security and
identity and access management

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:01.490
solutions to make sure that
access is appropriately

00:21:01.490 --> 00:21:05.300
controlled to a SAS application,
even though the customer is not

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:08.970
responsible for managing
anything below a certain level

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:11.580
in that stack.

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:16.800
And finally, in regards to
the overview of Google Cloud,

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:18.330
I mentioned earlier
that there were

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:21.150
a number of government
regulations, frameworks,

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:23.580
industry standards that were
relevant to health and life

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:24.630
sciences.

00:21:24.630 --> 00:21:28.170
Here is an overview of Google
Cloud's overall compliance

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:28.780
posture.

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:33.870
This is not an exhaustive
list of everything customers

00:21:33.870 --> 00:21:38.190
can do on Google Cloud,
but it is a representation

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:41.640
of frameworks that Google
Cloud adheres to, some of which

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:44.190
have self-attestation,
some of which

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:47.610
have external, third-party
attestation, and some of which

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:51.510
have specific contractual
indicators as well.

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:56.710
So at this point, I
want to transition focus

00:21:56.710 --> 00:22:01.060
to setting up and managing
health care workloads on Google

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:04.300
Cloud, and specifically,
how you can best

00:22:04.300 --> 00:22:06.760
manage your shared
responsibilities

00:22:06.760 --> 00:22:09.710
and adhere to the various
security, privacy,

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:13.060
and compliance requirements
that you need to.

00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:15.580
And one of the offerings
that we have in this space

00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:18.700
is called the health care
data protection suite.

00:22:18.700 --> 00:22:20.950
And the health care
data protection suite

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:25.000
is an integrated tool for
deploying, monitoring,

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:27.880
and auditing Google
Cloud environments

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:30.910
and workloads that are
running on Google Cloud.

00:22:30.910 --> 00:22:35.980
And by integrated, I mean there
is very little lag between

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:38.650
the deployment of the
resources themselves,

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:40.240
when they start
getting monitored,

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:42.820
and when they're-- the
monitoring data is available

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:47.350
for investigation, triage,
documentation, and so on.

00:22:47.350 --> 00:22:49.540
How this works at
a technical level--

00:22:49.540 --> 00:22:51.250
we have integrated
infrastructure

00:22:51.250 --> 00:22:55.240
as code deployment templates,
command line tools,

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:58.210
and continuous integration
continuous deployment pipelines

00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:01.900
to enable easy, verifiable,
and reproducible deployment

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:06.700
of Google Cloud architectures,
as well as common health care

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:08.570
and life sciences solutions.

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:12.490
So what this means is we have
some predefined templates

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:15.280
for common health care
workloads on Google Cloud

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:19.960
such as HIPAA line
deployments or R&amp;D platforms

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:24.070
that account for various
compliance guidance,

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:27.670
as well as technical best
practices for setting up

00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:29.320
Google Cloud Services.

00:23:29.320 --> 00:23:32.450
And there is a automation
for this deployment.

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:34.240
There's automation
for validating

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:37.510
that everything in these
[INAUDIBLE] code templates

00:23:37.510 --> 00:23:39.160
was deployed appropriately.

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:41.260
And then there is
continuous monitoring

00:23:41.260 --> 00:23:44.380
against a set of
defined policies

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:48.010
that are defined at the
time of resource creation.

00:23:48.010 --> 00:23:50.740
And having this suite
fully integrated

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:54.370
means that you can define your
workload and the monitoring

00:23:54.370 --> 00:23:55.750
policies side by side.

00:23:55.750 --> 00:23:59.710
You can ensure there's no or
minimal gaps in monitoring

00:23:59.710 --> 00:24:03.790
coverage and start scanning
for policy violations

00:24:03.790 --> 00:24:06.530
as soon as the
resources are deployed.

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:09.970
And this is a little bit more
useful than some other tool

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:12.850
suites where the monitoring
deployment and auditing are all

00:24:12.850 --> 00:24:14.470
separate steps.

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:17.170
In some of those
other workflows,

00:24:17.170 --> 00:24:19.690
the environment gets deployed.

00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:23.110
There can often be a lag of
several hours to several weeks

00:24:23.110 --> 00:24:25.240
between when the
resources are deployed

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:27.880
and when they begin to
be monitored and then

00:24:27.880 --> 00:24:30.430
lag between when the
monitoring data is available

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:34.720
and when it's available for
triage and investigation

00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:38.410
in any sort of UI interface.

00:24:38.410 --> 00:24:42.120
So if we take a look at
how this works in detail,

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:46.290
there is a suite of
configuration libraries

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:48.930
that we've made
available to our users.

00:24:48.930 --> 00:24:54.220
And what these allow users to
do is create deployment modules.

00:24:54.220 --> 00:24:56.890
So you can have deployment
modules for specific services,

00:24:56.890 --> 00:25:00.600
like BigQuery, Google Cloud
Storage, and Compute Engine

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:04.920
that tell the application
how these services should

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:06.840
be configured. where
they should be set up.

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:09.340
What the IM policy
should look like.

00:25:09.340 --> 00:25:12.032
What the monitoring
rules should look like.

00:25:12.032 --> 00:25:13.740
And then these are
all stored in the form

00:25:13.740 --> 00:25:16.120
of a declarative template.

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:18.550
And once this template
is ready to be deployed,

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:20.550
we have integration
with terraform

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:24.960
for continuous deployment,
continuous integration,

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:28.140
and automation of the
deployment practices.

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:31.950
And what this allows you to
do is do your code reviews

00:25:31.950 --> 00:25:34.470
on automation code.

00:25:34.470 --> 00:25:36.540
You don't have to wait
for the environment

00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:38.610
to be set up to
do the validation.

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:41.190
It also helps you set
up multiple instances

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:44.260
of the exact same infrastructure
for different purposes.

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:47.490
So you can imagine potentially
some GXP-related requirements

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:51.780
where you have to have identical
production development and test

00:25:51.780 --> 00:25:53.410
or QA environments.

00:25:53.410 --> 00:25:55.380
And this automated
template framework

00:25:55.380 --> 00:25:59.200
allows you to set these up
exactly equal to each other.

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:00.930
You don't have to rely
on making sure you

00:26:00.930 --> 00:26:03.210
click the right
buttons in the console

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:06.600
or run the right command line
arguments in the same order

00:26:06.600 --> 00:26:09.340
every time.

00:26:09.340 --> 00:26:11.440
Diving deeper into
these templates,

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:14.800
the deployment templates can be
written in a native Terraform

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:18.670
syntax or in our own
declarative YAML syntax that

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:20.213
compiles into Terraform.

00:26:20.213 --> 00:26:22.630
And different organizations
may have different preferences

00:26:22.630 --> 00:26:24.550
for how they use these tools.

00:26:24.550 --> 00:26:26.830
Organizations that
have Terraform code

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:30.520
already can reuse
that, or organizations

00:26:30.520 --> 00:26:33.130
that don't have
existing terraform code

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:35.800
and prefer a
declarative YAML syntax

00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:39.310
can start there and generate
the Terraform output.

00:26:39.310 --> 00:26:41.620
As I mentioned, these deployment
templates are modular.

00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:43.180
They can be linked
together to make

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:47.650
larger projects or organization
structures for the workloads.

00:26:47.650 --> 00:26:51.370
And they also have accounted
for resource-specific

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:52.640
configuration.

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:56.530
So where a specific resource
has a configuration parameter,

00:26:56.530 --> 00:26:59.260
you can account for that
specifically and not

00:26:59.260 --> 00:27:03.790
have to set it globally
after deployment.

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:07.570
So this example on the
slide is setting up

00:27:07.570 --> 00:27:10.910
a project for holding data.

00:27:10.910 --> 00:27:14.140
And what this is doing is it's
setting up a BigQuery data set

00:27:14.140 --> 00:27:18.430
with a data retention policy
to map a specific privacy

00:27:18.430 --> 00:27:19.870
or compliance requirement.

00:27:19.870 --> 00:27:23.420
It sets a data viewer
group, as well as

00:27:23.420 --> 00:27:25.900
a project owner
group that has access

00:27:25.900 --> 00:27:27.680
to all of the resources
within a project.

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:31.120
And essentially, once
this template is deployed,

00:27:31.120 --> 00:27:32.840
it will result in
a BigQuery data

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:38.230
set with all the appropriate
settings and controls on it.

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:42.750
And leaning on from this,
the resource deployment

00:27:42.750 --> 00:27:45.810
feeds directly into the
monitoring policies.

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:49.140
And the monitoring policies
can be auto-generated based

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:50.280
on the deployment template.

00:27:50.280 --> 00:27:54.090
So where there is a
setting for IAM policy

00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:57.030
in the deployment
template, the tools

00:27:57.030 --> 00:28:00.420
can infer that you
might want a monitoring

00:28:00.420 --> 00:28:05.430
policy to prevent changes
to that IAM configuration.

00:28:05.430 --> 00:28:09.730
Or the monitoring policies can
also be explicitly specified.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:11.520
So if you have a
very specific set

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:15.930
of allowable,
contractual requirements

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:20.160
with your customers and any one
of those settings is allowed,

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:21.780
you can set a
monitoring policy that

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:24.930
allows you to set any one
of those configurations

00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:28.860
but not deviate from
that approved set.

00:28:28.860 --> 00:28:31.920
By default, the scanners
for these monitoring

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:35.010
controls run at a
default 12-hour interval.

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:38.070
However, this can be
configured to any interval

00:28:38.070 --> 00:28:41.670
that you're comfortable
with in your organization.

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:44.580
The examples of available
monitoring policies

00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:47.490
are centered around compliance
requirements, specifically

00:28:47.490 --> 00:28:50.490
the security and privacy
compliance requirements.

00:28:50.490 --> 00:28:55.200
And some examples are access
control lists and IAM policies.

00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:57.900
Ensuring that your Cloud
audit logging settings

00:28:57.900 --> 00:28:59.520
are appropriate.

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:02.250
Making sure that the APIs
that have been enabled

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:06.540
in your project are appropriate
for what that project will

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:08.850
be doing with data.

00:29:08.850 --> 00:29:13.020
And going further down the
list, if there are specific data

00:29:13.020 --> 00:29:15.600
retention requirements or
data lifecycle requirements,

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:18.490
those can be
monitored for as well.

00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:20.430
So the example on
the left side is

00:29:20.430 --> 00:29:25.830
showing how to explicitly
specify monitoring requirements

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:28.290
in the configuration interface.

00:29:28.290 --> 00:29:30.840
And what this is
doing here is showing

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:34.810
that there's two controls
that are being monitored for.

00:29:34.810 --> 00:29:37.680
One is the policy
member domains,

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:40.470
which you are allowed
to have membership

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:43.630
within the food domain or
the bar domain, for example.

00:29:43.630 --> 00:29:46.380
And then the allowed
shared VPC host projects,

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:48.390
which are declared beneath.

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:50.250
And what these
monetary rules will

00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:55.500
do is if there is any deviation
from the two named policy

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:59.970
member domains or the two
VPC shared host projects,

00:29:59.970 --> 00:30:02.070
you will get an alert
in the monitoring

00:30:02.070 --> 00:30:07.300
toolset that claims that
there has been a deviation.

00:30:07.300 --> 00:30:09.970
And finally the last step
in this integrated process

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:11.950
is the auditing and reporting.

00:30:11.950 --> 00:30:14.020
So the data protection
dashboard here

00:30:14.020 --> 00:30:17.140
enables users to
visualize tracked policy

00:30:17.140 --> 00:30:20.110
violations captured by
these monitoring tools.

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:22.810
You can dig into the
policy violations,

00:30:22.810 --> 00:30:26.590
view the scan results
in raw detail,

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:30.830
as well as understand long-term
and short-term policy violation

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:31.330
trends.

00:30:31.330 --> 00:30:34.090
And having this suite
fully integrated

00:30:34.090 --> 00:30:37.840
helps maintain the context
all the way from deployment

00:30:37.840 --> 00:30:41.080
to the end of the process,
which is auditing, all

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:44.980
the way back to starting over
with redeployment or iterations

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:47.220
on top.

00:30:47.220 --> 00:30:50.510
So an example of a GCP
environment deployed

00:30:50.510 --> 00:30:52.910
with the data
protection suite is

00:30:52.910 --> 00:30:54.177
this HIPAA-aligned deployment.

00:30:54.177 --> 00:30:55.760
You can see that
there is a connection

00:30:55.760 --> 00:30:59.210
to an on-prem interface
that helps bring data

00:30:59.210 --> 00:31:00.820
to and from Google Cloud.

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:02.660
There is a networking
project that

00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:04.750
manages the
interconnects the routing

00:31:04.750 --> 00:31:07.880
and the firewall rules between
the on-prem and the Cloud

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:08.810
environment.

00:31:08.810 --> 00:31:11.940
And then there's
two Cloud projects.

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:15.880
One is for hosting the data
and application itself,

00:31:15.880 --> 00:31:18.290
and one is for the
security operations

00:31:18.290 --> 00:31:22.700
that run the monitoring tools,
as well as the auditing UI.

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:26.930
So this can be deployed in just
a couple lines of configuration

00:31:26.930 --> 00:31:30.510
and setup within an
organization in a few minutes.

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:33.110
And this is in contrast to
the several days or weeks

00:31:33.110 --> 00:31:35.030
it would take to get
the same environment set

00:31:35.030 --> 00:31:40.380
up and validated through
more manual means.

00:31:40.380 --> 00:31:43.050
So wrapping up on the
data protection suite

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:46.940
the main benefits here are
accelerated migration timeline,

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:50.130
decreased time to realizing
the value of Cloud,

00:31:50.130 --> 00:31:53.070
and pre-applying the best
practices for security

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:55.490
and compliance.

00:31:55.490 --> 00:31:58.640
If we look at one other
area of Google Cloud that

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:02.180
helps organizations meet their
privacy compliance requirements

00:32:02.180 --> 00:32:05.281
specifically, its
de-identification.

00:32:05.281 --> 00:32:10.320
de-identification is the removal
of personally identifying

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:15.090
information from data such
that you can no longer identify

00:32:15.090 --> 00:32:19.070
the individuals that the
data or data set links to

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:21.870
but that the data
retains utility

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:25.570
for research and commercial
development purposes.

00:32:25.570 --> 00:32:27.750
And both aspects are
important because it's

00:32:27.750 --> 00:32:30.040
important to maintain
patient privacy

00:32:30.040 --> 00:32:34.530
but also not delete the
research value of the data set

00:32:34.530 --> 00:32:36.650
in the process.

00:32:36.650 --> 00:32:41.500
De-identification is important
because it can prepare data

00:32:41.500 --> 00:32:43.840
for applications
that are outside

00:32:43.840 --> 00:32:45.910
of the traditional
clinical domain.

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:48.700
And some examples
of this are ensuring

00:32:48.700 --> 00:32:52.900
that data is private for
analytics for sharing securely

00:32:52.900 --> 00:32:54.970
with other organizations
and for using

00:32:54.970 --> 00:32:57.450
in application development.

00:32:57.450 --> 00:32:59.650
De-identification is
an important aspect

00:32:59.650 --> 00:33:04.150
of several privacy regulations,
and one of those is HIPAA.

00:33:04.150 --> 00:33:09.160
So revisiting HIPAA, it allows
for using de-identified data

00:33:09.160 --> 00:33:11.290
in secondary use cases.

00:33:11.290 --> 00:33:13.370
Obviously, there are
certain limitations here.

00:33:13.370 --> 00:33:17.560
But for the most part, once data
is de-identified and provably

00:33:17.560 --> 00:33:20.770
de-identified, it is
available for use outside

00:33:20.770 --> 00:33:22.840
of the clinical workflow.

00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:25.450
And HIPAA defines a couple
of different approaches

00:33:25.450 --> 00:33:27.950
to claiming that data
sets are identified.

00:33:27.950 --> 00:33:31.510
One is expert determination,
and the other is safe harbor.

00:33:31.510 --> 00:33:34.180
It's important to note
that while these different

00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:36.940
approaches have different
benefits depending

00:33:36.940 --> 00:33:39.550
on whether the data set is
structured, semi-structured,

00:33:39.550 --> 00:33:43.120
or unstructured, neither
method is definitively

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:49.560
guaranteed to eliminate
a re-identification risk.

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:52.460
So looking at the
identifiers that HIPAA

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:55.340
is most interested
in, specifically

00:33:55.340 --> 00:33:57.380
for the safe harbor
approach, you'll

00:33:57.380 --> 00:33:59.870
see that there's kind
of 18 categories that

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:01.980
are important to consider.

00:34:01.980 --> 00:34:05.210
Most of them are fairly
straightforward and easy

00:34:05.210 --> 00:34:08.630
to interpret why they may
be personally identifying,

00:34:08.630 --> 00:34:12.830
such as names, telephone
numbers, fax numbers, email

00:34:12.830 --> 00:34:16.280
addresses, websites,
faces, and so on.

00:34:16.280 --> 00:34:19.790
There are also some
identifiers that on the surface

00:34:19.790 --> 00:34:22.429
may not be immediately
identifying

00:34:22.429 --> 00:34:29.040
such as zip codes or cities,
as well as some other aspects.

00:34:29.040 --> 00:34:32.300
But you can imagine that if
a zip code is small enough

00:34:32.300 --> 00:34:34.610
or there are few enough
people within a particular zip

00:34:34.610 --> 00:34:36.830
code that
statistically, it could

00:34:36.830 --> 00:34:39.949
be used to indicate a
subset of population

00:34:39.949 --> 00:34:41.480
that the data may belong to.

00:34:41.480 --> 00:34:44.360
So that's why
HIPAA tends to have

00:34:44.360 --> 00:34:49.710
opinions on geographic
location de-identification.

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:53.389
This slide kind
of shows the value

00:34:53.389 --> 00:34:56.000
of maintaining research
value of a data set

00:34:56.000 --> 00:34:57.650
during de-identification.

00:34:57.650 --> 00:34:59.550
If we look at the
example on the left,

00:34:59.550 --> 00:35:02.000
we can see that
somebody's social security

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:04.250
number is in this message
as well as their phone

00:35:04.250 --> 00:35:06.000
number and their name.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:07.430
And then on the
right side, there

00:35:07.430 --> 00:35:09.830
are three different levels
of de-identification.

00:35:09.830 --> 00:35:12.170
The first level is just
included for completeness.

00:35:12.170 --> 00:35:14.630
It's essentially
deleting all of the data.

00:35:14.630 --> 00:35:16.400
We have fully
de-identified this message,

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:18.830
but we've also eliminated
its research value.

00:35:18.830 --> 00:35:23.870
The second example shows how
to remove all of the PII,

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:25.940
but also, at the
same time, remove all

00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:28.740
of the utility of this example.

00:35:28.740 --> 00:35:30.890
The bottom example is--

00:35:30.890 --> 00:35:33.518
retains some of the
PII, specifically

00:35:33.518 --> 00:35:35.060
the name and the
phone number, but it

00:35:35.060 --> 00:35:38.390
removes the especially sensitive
PII of the social security

00:35:38.390 --> 00:35:39.150
number.

00:35:39.150 --> 00:35:40.640
And in this case,
this example is

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:44.660
the most useful for
completing the task that

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:46.430
needs to be done
here-- in this case,

00:35:46.430 --> 00:35:48.740
helping the patient get
an appointment scheduled,

00:35:48.740 --> 00:35:53.240
but also ensuring that
their sensitive PII is not

00:35:53.240 --> 00:35:56.550
overexposed with their
social security number.

00:35:56.550 --> 00:35:58.310
So there are tools
on Google Cloud

00:35:58.310 --> 00:36:00.630
to help with the
de-identification.

00:36:00.630 --> 00:36:03.920
The health care API is our
data platform for health care

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:07.430
data in standardized formats
such as FHIR and DICOM.

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:11.120
This service has an integrated
de-identification tool

00:36:11.120 --> 00:36:15.350
that can be used for
de-identifying data sets,

00:36:15.350 --> 00:36:18.380
then joining and exporting them
to things like machine learning

00:36:18.380 --> 00:36:22.310
models or analytics
and NBI tools.

00:36:22.310 --> 00:36:24.770
And this is when
you want to analyze

00:36:24.770 --> 00:36:27.110
an entire data set in batch.

00:36:27.110 --> 00:36:32.330
There is also the ability to
process individual records

00:36:32.330 --> 00:36:34.050
through the
de-identification workflow.

00:36:34.050 --> 00:36:37.010
And this can be useful
for real-time applications

00:36:37.010 --> 00:36:40.490
like viewers or patient
data applications

00:36:40.490 --> 00:36:44.000
where patients and providers
are interacting in real time

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:46.550
with each other.

00:36:46.550 --> 00:36:51.220
This is a quick example of how
the de-identification tools

00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:52.000
work.

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:55.840
The FHIR format is a mixture
of structured and unstructured

00:36:55.840 --> 00:36:57.460
data in a key value format.

00:36:57.460 --> 00:37:00.010
So you can see that there
is some structured PII

00:37:00.010 --> 00:37:03.370
on the left side in this
original FHIR record,

00:37:03.370 --> 00:37:05.470
and there's also
some unstructured PII

00:37:05.470 --> 00:37:06.970
at the very bottom.

00:37:06.970 --> 00:37:09.250
Through the
de-identification tools,

00:37:09.250 --> 00:37:11.720
the FHIR record
has been processed.

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:14.470
The structured fields have
been removed or transformed,

00:37:14.470 --> 00:37:17.770
as have the unstructured fields.

00:37:17.770 --> 00:37:22.270
For organizations that
want a specific method

00:37:22.270 --> 00:37:25.000
of de-identification done
but also don't necessarily

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:28.120
have the technical
abilities in-house to do so,

00:37:28.120 --> 00:37:32.650
we have a program that pairs
partner services with customers

00:37:32.650 --> 00:37:35.690
to perform some of these
de-identification activities.

00:37:35.690 --> 00:37:37.450
And this can help
customers get access

00:37:37.450 --> 00:37:40.330
to de-identified
and validated data

00:37:40.330 --> 00:37:42.220
without necessarily
needing to hire

00:37:42.220 --> 00:37:46.570
specific technical or
privacy skills in-house.

00:37:46.570 --> 00:37:48.310
And with that, it's
a good segue way

00:37:48.310 --> 00:37:52.000
to say that there are partners
available for all aspects

00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:54.910
of the security and privacy
compliance workflows,

00:37:54.910 --> 00:37:56.900
not just de-identification.

00:37:56.900 --> 00:38:00.220
This slide shows a
quick picture overview

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:03.430
of some of our partners in
this space, many of whom

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:05.440
you may already have
relationships with.

00:38:05.440 --> 00:38:08.410
So it's important to note
that there is a large partner

00:38:08.410 --> 00:38:11.710
ecosystem on Google Cloud
for the health care industry

00:38:11.710 --> 00:38:14.740
when it comes to security
and privacy compliance.

00:38:14.740 --> 00:38:18.090
And with that, thank you
for joining this session.

00:38:18.090 --> 00:38:20.850
I hope you learned
something useful.

